WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.380
Every alert comes with a severity — High, Medium, Low. It's tempting to treat that as the one number that tells you how urgently to act.

2
00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.820
Treating severity as the whole story is a mistake. Let's see why.

3
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.330
Severity is usually pre-assigned by the detection rule itself.

4
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:25.650
It tells you how the rule's designer categorized this type of behavior in general — not how serious this specific instance is right now.

5
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:31.070
Picture two alerts, both labeled Medium, arriving at the same moment.

6
00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:38.340
One: a single failed login on a shared, read-only reporting account with no access to anything sensitive.

7
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:48.300
The other: a single failed login on a domain administrator account that has never failed a login in years. Same label — but nobody would treat them equally.

8
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:56.340
A mature SOC reads three numbers, not one. Severity — how the rule categorizes this type of behavior.

9
00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:01.880
Confidence — how unusual this specific instance actually is for this account or host.

10
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:07.710
And risk — how much damage a real compromise of this specific asset or identity would cause.

11
00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:12.840
Risk extends the triage thinking you already know: likelihood times impact.

12
00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:23.280
Platforms like CrowdStrike, Splunk, and Sentinel add a layer on top — risk that accumulates on an entity as suspicious events pile up on the same user or host.

13
00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.480
So don't let a single severity label set your priority.

14
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:33.190
Combine severity, confidence, and risk — and work the alert that can actually hurt you most.
